  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry weather returns across the board through the upcoming week as \nmid-level ridging builds across the eastern half of the country and \nexpansive surface high pressure does the same. The column turns bone \ndry with ensemble mean PWATs a quarter inch or less. Temperatures \nwill gradually moderate as well, but the notable warm advection \ndoesn't come until the end of the week when the surface high that \ninitially sits overhead slides east and promotes southerly gradient \nflow. We will see some decent diurnal temperature swings as light \nwind and clearing skies allow for efficient radiational cooling and \na good probability of a couple consecutive nights of lows in the 20s \nduring the first half of the week. But, by the time late week comes \naround, NBM probs for highs >60 degrees ramp up quite high to 60-\n90%, incrementally inching north each day starting Wednesday.\n\nWe'll have to look toward sometime next weekend to see the next \npotential for rain to return as ridging breaks down, but that's when \nwe start to introduce disagreement with how much the ridge breaks \ndown/how far east a Midwest trough can penetrate. Ensembles have \ntrended later with arrival of rain with a faster solution now \nsometime Sunday morning but other solutions converging around Sunday \nnight. The former would also lend to cooler highs than currently \nforecast on Sunday with the latter a warmer outcome.",
